course: dictionary
lesson: 5729
title: Clues in a sentence
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name things you may never have seen, such as lorikeet. The sentence around the word gives you clues. «Lorikeets have brush-tipped tongues for licking nectar and pollen from flowers, and they are noisy visitors to gardens in eastern Australia.» Licking nectar from flowers and visiting gardens tells you a lorikeet is a bird that lives around flowers.
explain: Look for describing words. Lorikeets are called noisy, so they make a lot of sound. Your dictionary adds that they are very brightly coloured parrots. Put the clues together and you can picture the bird before you check the meaning.
explain: Then test your meaning in the sentence: «Lorikeets are small, bright parrots that lick nectar from flowers.» It still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
fact: A lorikeet is a small, very brightly coloured parrot.
fact: Lorikeets come from Australia, New Guinea and nearby islands.
fact: They feed on nectar and pollen, using a brush-tipped tongue.
fact: Rainbow lorikeets have a blue head, green back and orange chest, and are common in Australian gardens and parks.
fact: In New Zealand and Western Australia, lorikeets can be pests, because they were brought there and compete with local birds.
exercise: «Lorikeets have brush-tipped tongues for licking nectar and pollen from flowers.» What do lorikeets eat? | *Nectar and pollen | Seeds from the ground | Fish from rivers | Insects from tree bark
exercise: What is a lorikeet? | A brown animal that lives underground | *A small, very brightly coloured parrot | A large grey fish | A type of flower
exercise: «They are noisy visitors to gardens in eastern Australia.» Find and copy the word that tells you lorikeets are loud. | eastern | *noisy | gardens | Australia
test: «A flock of lorikeets flew into the park, bright as rainbows.» What does a flock of lorikeets mean? | A single lorikeet on a branch | A wild dog pack | *A group of lorikeets together | A river in Australia
test: Why can lorikeets be pests in New Zealand? | *Because they were brought there and compete with local birds | Because they only eat dead insects | Because they live underground there | Because they sing too quietly to hear
exam: Ruby thinks a lorikeet must be a fish because the word sounds watery, so what is it really, using the sentence clues? | A flower that grows in gardens | A dog that lives in parks | *A brightly coloured parrot that feeds on nectar | A fish that swims in rivers
